    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "AcceptanceTest is the only test class, and it passes. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI moved date-derived owner values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starts 1 July, named for the calendar year it ends in, e.g. FY26), touching four files:\n\n1. **`FiscalYear.java`** (new) \u2014 a small, cohesive helper with three tiny statics: `endYear(date)`, `label(date)` \u2192 `\"FY<YY>\"`, and `elapsed(from, to)`. All the new logic lives here so nothing else had to grow.\n\n2. **`MembershipLevel.java`** \u2014 two minimal edits: the tenure `+3` condition now uses `tenureFiscalYears(owner) > 1` instead of `> 365` days, and the private helper delegates to `FiscalYear.elapsed(...)`. Dropped the now-unused `ChronoUnit` import and updated the doc.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the `membershipNumber` year segment now derives from `FiscalYear.endYear(...)` rather than the calendar year, and a new `fiscalYear` mapping returns `FiscalYear.label(...)` of the (already business-day-adjusted) registration date.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `fiscalYear` response field and updated the `membershipNumber` description.\n\nStructural impact stayed low: the substantive logic is isolated in a brand-new zero-context class, and every edit to an existing method was a single-line, complexity-neutral swap. The acceptance suite (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`) is green \u2014 the new `coreReturnsFiscalYear` check passes and the audit log confirms the membership number now uses the fiscal year (`...-M27` for a Sept 2026 registration).",
